      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `semver_compare(a, b)` now follows the README contract. Both public tests pass with `python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py`.\n\n- **Parsing:** each version must be exactly `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, with every part either `0` or a plain ASCII number that doesn't start with a zero. The whole string has to match, so a trailing newline doesn't slip through.\n- **Errors:** anything that isn't a string raises `TypeError`. A string in any other format raises `ValueError`.\n- **Comparison:** the three numbers are compared in order (major, then minor, then patch), returning `-1`, `0` or `1`.\n\nI also ran some quick checks of my own:\n- `1.10.0` compares as greater than `1.9.9`, and `2.0.0` as less than `10.0.0`.\n- `ValueError` is raised for `1.2`, `1.02.3`, `1.2.3-rc`, surrounding whitespace, a leading `+`, full-width digits and `1..3`.\n- `TypeError` is raised for `None`, integers and bytes.\n\nDONE: Rewrote `semver_compare` to parse strict `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` strings (ValueError for bad strings, TypeError for non-strings) and compare them as number tuples; public tests pass.",
